type: software
title: "How badly can column-pivoted QR choose rows of an orthonormal basis? The sharp stretched-exponential worst case"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. Column-pivoted QR (QRCP) applied to Q*, where Q is an N x k matrix with orthonormal columns, is the standard way to select k interpolation points (for example in DEIM). Its quality is gamma = ||Q(I,:)^{-1}||_2; a selection with gamma <= sqrt(k(N-k+1)) always exists, while the classical guarantee for QRCP grows like 2^k. Chen, Liu, He and Dong answered Problem 4.3 of Amsel et al. negatively, and their construction already gives growth exp(c sqrt k). This work determines the worst case on the logarithmic scale: if the coherence is at most H, every legal QRCP selection has gamma <= sqrt(e(N-k+1)) exp(C(H) sqrt k), with an explicit constant C(H) (C(1) = 2), and matching examples show that C(H) cannot be lowered (at H = 1 under the smallest-label tie rule). Without any assumption the worst case is exp(Theta(sqrt(k log(N/k)))) once N >= k^(2+eps). An a posteriori bound from the observed pivots has an optimal coefficient. Exact arithmetic and worst cases only."
